\fs96 \cf2 Parameterization\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Many programming languages offer systems for writing generic or polymorphic code, which let you write code once, and generate efficient, specialized code at compile time.\
Mojo's compile-time parameter system lets you define reusable code. A parameter is a compile-time input to a struct or function. Parameters appear in square brackets after the struct or function name. Parameters can take ordinary values, like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  multiplier\cf5 [\cf14 factor\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ](\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  x \cf5 *\cf14  factor\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  times_ten \cf5 =\cf14  multiplier\cf5 [\cf16 10\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     x10 \cf5 =\cf14  times_ten\cf5 (\cf16 3\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Parameters can also take types as values, so you can write generic code that works for multiple data types:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyList\cf5 [\cf14 T\cf5 :\cf14  AnyType\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # ... implementation omitted\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  l \cf5 =\cf14  MyList\cf5 [\cf14 Int\cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo's parameters are similar to C++ template parameters or Rust generic parameters.\
In Mojo, "parameter" and "parameter expression" refer to compile-time values, and "argument" and "expression" refer to dynamic values\'97which can be evaluated either at compile time or at run time. This usage of "parameter" is probably different from what you're used to from other languages, where "parameter" and "argument" are often used interchangeably.\
In addition to parameterizing structs and functions, you can also define parameterized 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Parameterized functions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To define a parameterized function, add parameters in square brackets ahead of the argument list. Each parameter is formatted just like an argument: a parameter name, followed by a colon and a type. In the following example, the function has a single parameter, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 count
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  repeat\cf5 [\cf14 count\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ](\cf14 msg\cf5 :\cf14  String\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  \cf5 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 range\cf5 (\cf14 count\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf14 msg\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/metaprogramming/comptime-evaluation/#comptime-for"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 comptime}} keyword shown here causes the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loop to be fully unrolled at compile time. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  requires the loop limits to be known at compile time. Since 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 count
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a parameter, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 range(count)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can be calculated at compile time.\
Calling a parameterized function, you provide values for the parameters, just like function arguments:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 repeat\cf5 [\cf16 3\cf5 ](\cf19 "Hello"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 Hello\cb1 \
\cb13 Hello\cb1 \
\cb13 Hello\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The compiler resolves the parameter values during compilation, and creates a concrete version of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 repeat[]()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function for each unique parameter value. After resolving the parameter values and unrolling the loop, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 repeat[3]()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function would be roughly equivalent to this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 repeat_3\cf5 (\cf14 msg\cf5 :\cf14  String\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 msg\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 msg\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 msg\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 This doesn't represent actual code generated by the compiler. By the time parameters are resolved, Mojo code has already been transformed to an intermediate representation in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://mlir.llvm.org/"}}{\fldrslt \ul MLIR}}.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If the compiler can't resolve all parameter values to constant values, compilation fails.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Overloading on parameters\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Functions and methods can be overloaded on their parameter signatures. For information on overload resolution, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/functions/#overloaded-functions"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Overloaded functions}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Parameters at a glance\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Parameters to a function or struct appear in square brackets after a function or struct name. Parameters always require type annotations.\
When you're looking at a function or struct signature, you may see some special characters such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 /
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 *
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in the parameter list. Here's an example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  my_sort\cf5 [\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # infer-only parameters\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     dtype\cf5 :\cf14  DType\cf5 ,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     width\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 //,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # positional-only parameter\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     values\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  width\cf5 ],\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 /,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # positional-or-keyword parameter\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     compare\cf5 :\cf14  \cf5 def(\cf14 Scalar\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 ],\cf14  Scalar\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 ])\cf14  raises \cf5 ->\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 *,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # keyword-only parameter\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     reverse\cf5 :\cf14  Bool \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 False\cf5 ,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 ]()\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  width\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here's a quick overview of the special characters in the parameter list:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Double slash (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 //
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ): parameters declared before the double slash are \cf5 \ul \ulc5 infer-only parameters\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Slash (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 /
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ): parameters declared before a slash are positional-only parameters. Positional-only and keyword-only parameters follow the same rules as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/functions/#positional-only-and-keyword-only-arguments"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul positional-only and keyword-only arguments}}.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A parameter name prefixed with a star, like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 *Types
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  identifies a \cf5 \ul variadic parameter\cf2 \ulnone  (not shown in the example above). Any parameters following the variadic parameter are keyword-only.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Star (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 *
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ): in a parameter list with no variadic parameter, a star by itself indicates that the following parameters are keyword-only parameters.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
An equals sign (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 =
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) introduces a default value for an \cf5 \ul optional parameter\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Parameters and generics\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 "Generics" refers to functions that can act on multiple types of values, or containers that can hold multiple types of values. For example, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/list/List/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul List}}, can hold different types of values, so you can have a list of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values, or a list of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values.\
In Mojo, generics use parameters to specify types. For example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  takes a type parameter, so a vector of integers is written 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List[Int]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . So all generics use parameters, but not everything that uses parameters is a generic.\
For example, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 repeat[]()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function in the previous section includes parameter of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and an argument of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . It's parameterized, but not generic. A generic function or struct is parameterized on type. For example, we could rewrite 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 repeat[]()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to take any type of argument that conforms to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/format/Writable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Writable}} trait:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  repeat\cf5 [\cf14 MsgType\cf5 :\cf14  Writable\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 //,\cf14  count\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ](\cf14 msg\cf5 :\cf14  MsgType\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  \cf5 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 range\cf5 (\cf14 count\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf14 msg\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # MsgType is always inferred, so first positional keyword `2` is\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # passed to `count`\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     repeat\cf5 [\cf16 2\cf5 ](\cf16 42\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 42\cb1 \
\cb13 42\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 This updated function takes any 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type, so you can pass it an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Bool
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value.\
Note that there's a double-slash (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 //
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) in the parameter list after 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MsgType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , to show that it's an \cf5 \ul infer-only parameter\cf2 \ulnone , so you don't need to specify it explicitly. Instead, the compiler sees that the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 msg
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument is an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and infers the type from the value.\
Mojo's support for generics is still early. You can write generic functions like this using traits and parameters. You can also write generic collections like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Dict
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . For more information, see the section on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/generics/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul generics}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Parameterized structs\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can also add parameters to structs. You can use parameterized structs to build generic collections. For example, a generic array type might include code like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 GenericArray\cf5 [\cf14 ElementType\cf5 :\cf14  Copyable \cf5 &\cf14  ImplicitlyDestructible\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  data\cf5 :\cf14  UnsafePointer\cf5 [\cf14 Self\cf5 .\cf14 ElementType\cf5 ,\cf14  MutExternalOrigin\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  size\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 var\cf14  \cf5 *\cf14 elements\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 ElementType\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 size \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 len\cf5 (\cf14 elements\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 data \cf5 =\cf14  alloc\cf5 [\cf14 Self\cf5 .\cf14 ElementType\cf5 ](\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 range\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             \cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 data \cf5 +\cf14  i\cf5 ).\cf14 init_pointee_move\cf5 (\cf14 elements\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 ].\cf14 copy\cf5 ())\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __del__\cf5 (deinit\cf14  self\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 range\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             \cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 data \cf5 +\cf14  i\cf5 ).\cf14 destroy_pointee\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 data\cf5 .\cf14 free\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __getitem__\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 ,\cf14  i\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 )\cf14  raises \cf5 ->\cf14  \cf5 ref[\cf14 self\cf5 ]\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 ElementType\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 if\cf14  i \cf5 <\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             \cf5 return\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf14 data\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 else:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             \cf5 raise\cf14  Error\cf5 (\cf19 "Out of bounds"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This struct has a single parameter, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ElementType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which is a placeholder for the data type you want to store in the array, sometimes called a type parameter. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ElementType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  conforms to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/value/Copyable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Copyable}} trait and therefore to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/value/Movable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Movable}} trait.\
As with parameterized functions, you need to pass in parameter values when you use a parameterized struct. In this case, when you create an instance of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 GenericArray
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you need to specify the type you want to store, like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . (This is a little confusing, because the parameter value you're passing in this case is a type. That's OK: a Mojo type is a valid compile-time value.)\
You'll see that 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Self.ElementType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is used throughout the struct where you'd usually see a type name. For example, as the formal type for the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 elements
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in the constructor, and the return type of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __getitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method.\
Here's an example of using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 GenericArray
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  array \cf5 =\cf14  GenericArray\cf5 (\cf16 1\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 2\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 3\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 4\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 range\cf5 (\cf14 array\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     end \cf5 =\cf14  \cf19 ", "\cf14  \cf5 if\cf14  i \cf5 <\cf14  array\cf5 .\cf14 size \cf5 -\cf14  \cf16 1\cf14  \cf5 else\cf14  \cf19 "\\n"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 array\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 ],\cf14  end\cf5 =\cf14 end\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 1, 2, 3, 4\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A parameterized struct can use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type to represent a concrete instance of the struct (that is, with all its parameters specified). For example, you could add a static factory method to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 GenericArray
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with the following signature:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 GenericArray\cf5 [\cf14 ElementType\cf5 :\cf14  Copyable \cf5 &\cf14  ImplicitlyDestructible\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 @staticmethod\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 splat\cf5 (\cf14 count\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  value\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 ElementType\cf5 )\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  Self\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 # Create a new array with count instances of the given value\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is equivalent to writing 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 GenericArray[Self.ElementType]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . That is, you can call the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 splat()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 GenericArray\cf5 [\cf14 Float64\cf5 ].\cf14 splat\cf5 (\cf16 8\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 0\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The method returns an instance of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 GenericArray[Float64]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Referencing struct parameters\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 As shown in the previous section, you reference a struct parameter using dot syntax, just like a struct method or field (for example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Self.ElementType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
This struct parameter access works anywhere, not just inside a struct's methods. You can access parameters as attributes on the type itself:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 on_type\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 float32\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 2\cf5 ].\cf14 size\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # prints 2\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Or as attributes on an instance of the type:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 on_instance\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  x \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 int32\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 2\cf5 ](\cf16 4\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 8\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 x\cf5 .\cf14 dtype\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # prints int32\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  members\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can also define 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values as members of a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 struct
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 trait
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  declaration:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Circle\cf5 [\cf14 radius\cf5 :\cf14  Float64\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  pi \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 3.14159265359\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  circumference \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 2\cf14  \cf5 *\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 pi \cf5 *\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 radius\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 These 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members have a number of uses:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Constant values specific to the type.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Constant values calculated based on the struct's parameters.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Associated types based on the struct's parameters.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The difference between parameters and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members is that parameter values are specified by the user, but 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members represent either constant values or values derived from the input parameters.\
A trait can declare a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  member, which must be defined by all conforming structs.\
Referencing 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members works just like referencing struct parameters\'97you can reference a member using dot syntax (such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Self.IteratorType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs35 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs40 \cf2 \cb1  members as enumerations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Some Mojo types use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members to express enumerations. For example, the following code defines a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Sentiment
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type that defines 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constants for different sentiment values:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @fieldwise_init\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Sentiment\cf5 (\cf14 Equatable\cf5 ,\cf14  ImplicitlyCopyable\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  _value\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  NEGATIVE \cf5 =\cf14  Sentiment\cf5 (\cf16 0\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  NEUTRAL \cf5 =\cf14  Sentiment\cf5 (\cf16 1\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  POSITIVE \cf5 =\cf14  Sentiment\cf5 (\cf16 2\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __eq__\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 ,\cf14  other\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 )\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  Bool\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 return\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf14 _value \cf5 ==\cf14  other\cf5 .\cf14 _value\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __ne__\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 ,\cf14  other\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 )\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  Bool\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 return\cf14  \cf5 not\cf14  \cf5 (\cf14 self \cf5 ==\cf14  other\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 is_happy\cf5 (\cf14 s\cf5 :\cf14  Sentiment\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 if\cf14  s \cf5 ==\cf14  Sentiment\cf5 .\cf14 POSITIVE\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf19 "Yes. \uc0\u55357 \u56832 "\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 else:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf19 "No. \uc0\u9785 \u65039 "\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This pattern provides a type-safe enumeration.\
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/dtype/DType/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul DType}} struct implements a simple enum using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members like this. This allows clients to use values like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType.float32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in parameter expressions or runtime expressions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs35 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs40 \cf2 \cb1  members as associated types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Associated types are a common use for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members. For example, a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List[T]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct holds values of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 T
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The list's 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __iter__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method returns a list iterator that returns values of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 T
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  uses a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  member, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 IteratorType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , to define the type of the returned iterator.\
The following code excerpt shows a simplified version of some of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  code, showing the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and its associated 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 IteratorType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @fieldwise_init\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 _ListIter\cf5 [\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 mut:\cf14  Bool\cf5 ,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 //,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     T\cf5 :\cf14  Copyable\cf5 ,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     origin\cf5 :\cf14  Origin\cf5 [mut],\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 ](\cf14 ImplicitlyCopyable\cf5 ,\cf14  Iterable\cf5 ,\cf14  Iterator\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  Element \cf5 =\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 T  \cf6 # Required by the Iterator trait\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  index\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  src\cf5 :\cf14  Pointer\cf5 [\cf14 List\cf5 [\cf14 Self\cf5 .\cf14 Element\cf5 ],\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 origin\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf6 # ... implementation omitted\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 List\cf5 [\cf14 T\cf5 :\cf14  Copyable\cf5 ](\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     Boolable\cf5 ,\cf14  Copyable\cf5 ,\cf14  Defaultable\cf5 ,\cf14  Iterable\cf5 ,\cf14  Sized\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  IteratorType\cf5 [\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         iterable_mut\cf5 :\cf14  Bool\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 //,\cf14  iterable_origin\cf5 :\cf14  Origin\cf5 [\cf14 iterable_mut\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 ]:\cf14  Iterator \cf5 =\cf14  _ListIter\cf5 [\cf14 Self\cf5 .\cf14 T\cf5 ,\cf14  iterable_origin\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf6 # ... code omitted\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __iter__\cf5 (ref\cf14  self\cf5 )\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 IteratorType\cf5 [\cf14 origin_of\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 )]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 return\cf14  \cf5 \{\cf16 0\cf5 ,\cf14  Pointer\cf5 (\cf14 to\cf5 =\cf14 self\cf5 )\}\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf6 # ... code omitted\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 IteratorType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  member is parameterized on an origin, so it can represent both mutable and immutable iterators.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Struct methods\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A struct's method can take its own parameters. For example, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD.slice()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method takes a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 size
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  m \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 int32\cf5 ](\cf16 1\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 3\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 5\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 7\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  n \cf5 =\cf14  m\cf5 .\cf15 slice\cf5 [\cf16 2\cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 print(\cf14 n\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # prints [1, 3]\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 A struct's lifecycle methods (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) are an exception to this rule\'97they can't take parameters.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Case study: the SIMD type\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 For a real-world example of a parameterized type, let's look at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/simd/SIMD/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul SIMD}} type from Mojo's standard library.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_instruction,_multiple_data"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Single instruction, multiple data (SIMD)}} is a parallel processing technology built into many modern CPUs, GPUs, and custom accelerators. SIMD allows you to perform a single operation on multiple pieces of data at once. For example, if you want to take the square root of each element in an array, you can use SIMD to parallelize the work.\
Processors implement SIMD using low-level vector registers in hardware that hold multiple instances of a scalar data type. To use the SIMD instructions on these processors, the data must be shaped into the proper SIMD width (data type) and length (vector size). Processors may support 512-bit or longer SIMD vectors, and support many data types from 8-bit integers to 64-bit floating point numbers, so it's not practical to define all of the possible SIMD variations.\
Mojo's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/simd/SIMD/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul SIMD}} type (defined as a struct) exposes the common SIMD operations through its methods, and takes the SIMD data type and size values as parameters. This allows you to directly map your data to the SIMD vectors on any hardware.\
Here's a cut-down (non-functional) version of Mojo's 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type definition:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 :\cf14  DType\cf5 ,\cf14  size\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  value\cf5 :\cf14  \'85 \cf6 # Some low-level MLIR stuff here\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf6 # Create a new SIMD from a number of scalars\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 *\cf14 elems\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 Self\cf5 .\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 1\cf5 ]):\cf14   \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf6 # Fill a SIMD with a duplicated scalar value.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 @staticmethod\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 splat\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 Self\cf5 .\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 1\cf5 ])\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 Self\cf5 .\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 ]:\cf14  \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf6 # Cast the elements of the SIMD to a different elt type.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  cast\cf5 [\cf14 target\cf5 :\cf14  DType\cf5 ](\cf14 self\cf5 )\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 target\cf5 ,\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 ]:\cf14  \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf6 # Many standard operators are supported.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __add__\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 ,\cf14  rhs\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 )\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  Self\cf5 :\cf14  \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 So you can create and use a SIMD vector like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  vector \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 int16\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 4\cf5 ](\cf16 1\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 2\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 3\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 4\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 vector \cf5 =\cf14  vector \cf5 *\cf14  vector\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 range\cf5 (\cf16 4\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 vector\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 ],\cf14  end\cf5 =\cf19 " "\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 1 4 9 16\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 As you can see, a simple arithmetic operator like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 *
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  applied to a pair of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  vector operates on the corresponding elements in each vector.\
Defining each SIMD variant with parameters is great for code reuse because the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type can express all the different vector variants statically, instead of requiring the language to pre-define every variant.\
Because 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a parameterized type, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument in its functions carries those parameters\'97the full type name is 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD[type, size]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Although it's valid to write this out (as shown in the return type of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 splat()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ), this can be verbose, so we recommend using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type (from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://peps.python.org/pep-0673/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul PEP673}}) like the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __add__
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  example does.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Using parameterized types and functions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can use parameterized types and functions by passing values to the parameters in square brackets. For example, for the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type above, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 dtype
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  specifies the data type and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 size
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  specifies the length of the SIMD vector (which must be a power of 2):\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # Make a vector of 4 floats.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  small_vec \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 float32\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 4\cf5 ](\cf16 1.0\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 2.0\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 3.0\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 4.0\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf6 # Make a big vector containing 1.0 in float16 format.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  big_vec \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 float16\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 32\cf5 ](\cf16 1.0\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf6 # Do some math and convert the elements to float32.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  bigger_vec \cf5 =\cf14  \cf5 (\cf14 big_vec \cf5 +\cf14  big_vec\cf5 ).\cf14 cast\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 float32\cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf6 # You can write types out explicitly if you want of course.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  bigger_vec2\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 float32\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 32\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  bigger_vec\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf19 "small_vec DType:"\cf5 ,\cf14  small_vec\cf5 .\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf19 "size:"\cf5 ,\cf14  small_vec\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf19 "bigger_vec2 DType:"\cf5 ,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         bigger_vec2\cf5 .\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf19 "size:"\cf5 ,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         bigger_vec2\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 ,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 small_vec DType: float32 size: 4\cb1 \
\cb13 bigger_vec2 DType: float32 size: 32\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Note that the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 cast()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method also needs a parameter to specify the type you want from the cast (the method definition above expects a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 target
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter value). Thus, just as the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct is a generic type definition, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 cast()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method is a generic method definition. At compile time, the compiler creates a concrete version of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 cast()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method with the target parameter bound to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType.float32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
The code above shows the use of concrete types (that is, the parameters are all bound to known values). But the major power of parameters comes from the ability to define parameterized algorithms and types (code that uses the parameter values). For example, here's how to define a parameterized algorithm with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Scalar
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that is datatype agnostic:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 math \cf5 import\cf14  sqrt\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  rsqrt\cf5 [\cf14 dt\cf5 :\cf14  DType\cf5 ](\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  Scalar\cf5 [\cf14 dt\cf5 ])\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  Scalar\cf5 [\cf14 dt\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  \cf16 1\cf14  \cf5 /\cf14  sqrt\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  v \cf5 =\cf14  Scalar\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 float16\cf5 ](\cf16 42\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 rsqrt\cf5 (\cf14 v\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 0.154296875\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Parameter inference\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The Mojo compiler can often infer parameter values, so you don't always have to specify them. For example, in the previous section, this is how we called the parameterized 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 rsqrt()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  v \cf5 =\cf14  Scalar\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 float16\cf5 ](\cf16 42\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 print(\cf14 rsqrt\cf5 (\cf14 v\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The compiler infers the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 dt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter based on the type of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 v
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value passed into it, as if you wrote 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 rsqrt[DType.float16](v)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  explicitly. Figure 1 shows a mental model for how parameter inference works.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 1. Parameter inference\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Parameter inference can seem a little confusing: it might seem like the compiler is inferring compile-time parameter values from run-time argument values. But in fact it's inferring parameters from the statically-known types of the arguments.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb20 Inference failures\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb20 If parameter inference fails, the compiler will report an error, usually "failed to infer parameter 'param_name'". Unfortunately, the compiler also sometimes reports this error incorrectly, for example, when the actual error is a type mismatch. In these cases, specifying the missing parameters explicitly will often allow Mojo to report the correct error.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo can also infer the values of struct parameters from the arguments passed to a constructor or static method.\
For example, consider the following struct:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 One\cf5 [\cf14 Type\cf5 :\cf14  Writable \cf5 &\cf14  Copyable\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  value\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 Type\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  value\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 Type\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 value \cf5 =\cf14  value\cf5 .\cf14 copy\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 use_one\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     s1 \cf5 =\cf14  One\cf5 (\cf16 123\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # equivalent to One[Int](123)\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     s2 \cf5 =\cf14  One\cf5 (\cf19 "Hello"\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # equivalent to One[String]("Hello")\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Note that you can create an instance of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 One
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  without specifying the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Type
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter\'97Mojo can infer it from the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 value
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument.\
You can also infer parameters from a parameterized type passed to a constructor or static method:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Two\cf5 [\cf14 Type\cf5 :\cf14  Writable \cf5 &\cf14  Copyable\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  val1\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 Type\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  val2\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 Type\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  one\cf5 :\cf14  One\cf5 [\cf14 Self\cf5 .\cf14 Type\cf5 ],\cf14  another\cf5 :\cf14  One\cf5 [\cf14 Self\cf5 .\cf14 Type\cf5 ]):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 val1 \cf5 =\cf14  one\cf5 .\cf14 value\cf5 .\cf14 copy\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 val2 \cf5 =\cf14  another\cf5 .\cf14 value\cf5 .\cf14 copy\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf14 String\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 val1\cf5 ),\cf14  String\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 val2\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 @staticmethod\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 fire\cf5 (\cf14 thing1\cf5 :\cf14  One\cf5 [\cf14 Self\cf5 .\cf14 Type\cf5 ],\cf14  thing2\cf5 :\cf14  One\cf5 [\cf14 Self\cf5 .\cf14 Type\cf5 ]):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf19 "\uc0\u55357 \u56613 "\cf5 ,\cf14  String\cf5 (\cf14 thing1\cf5 .\cf14 value\cf5 ),\cf14  String\cf5 (\cf14 thing2\cf5 .\cf14 value\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 use_two\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     s3 \cf5 =\cf14  Two\cf5 (\cf14 One\cf5 (\cf19 "infer"\cf5 ),\cf14  One\cf5 (\cf19 "me"\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     Two\cf5 .\cf14 fire\cf5 (\cf14 One\cf5 (\cf16 1\cf5 ),\cf14  One\cf5 (\cf16 2\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # Two.fire(One("mixed"), One(0)) # Error: parameter inferred to two\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13                                      \cf6 # different values\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13 use_two\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 infer me\cb1 \
\cb13 \uc0\u55357 \u56613  1 2\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf10 \cb12 Two
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  takes a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Type
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter, and its constructor takes values of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 One[Type]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . When constructing an instance of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Two
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you don't need to specify the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Type
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter, since it can be inferred from the arguments.\
Similarly, the static 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 fire()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method takes values of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 One[Type]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , so Mojo can infer the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Type
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value at compile time. Note that passing two instances of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 One
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with different types doesn't work.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 If you're familiar with C++, you may recognize this as similar to Class Template Argument Deduction (CTAD).\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Parameter declarations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When you declare parameters on a struct or function, you have many of the same options as you have with arguments\'97you can define optional parameters with default values; keyword-only parameters; and variadic parameters.\
In addition, you can define infer-only parameters, which provide a flexible way of defining dependencies between parameterized types.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Optional parameters and keyword parameters\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Just as you can specify {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/functions/#optional-arguments"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul optional arguments}} in function signatures, you can also define an optional parameter by giving it a default value.\
You can also pass parameters by keyword, just like you can use {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/functions/#keyword-arguments"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul keyword arguments}}. For a function or struct with multiple optional parameters, using keywords allows you to pass only the parameters you want to specify, regardless of their position in the function signature.\
For example, here's a function with two parameters, each with a default value:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  speak\cf5 [\cf14 a\cf5 :\cf14  Int \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 3\cf5 ,\cf14  msg\cf5 :\cf14  String \cf5 =\cf14  \cf19 "woof"\cf5 ]():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 msg\cf5 ,\cf14  a\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 use_defaults\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     speak\cf5 ()\cf14   \cf6 # prints 'woof 3'\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     speak\cf5 [\cf16 5\cf5 ]()\cf14   \cf6 # prints 'woof 5'\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     speak\cf5 [\cf16 7\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf19 "meow"\cf5 ]()\cf14   \cf6 # prints 'meow 7'\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     speak\cf5 [\cf14 msg\cf5 =\cf19 "baaa"\cf5 ]()\cf14   \cf6 # prints 'baaa 3'\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Recall that when a parameterized function is called, Mojo can \cf5 \ul infer the parameter values\cf2 \ulnone . That is, it can determine its parameter values from the parameters attached to an argument. If the parameterized function also has a default value defined, then the inferred parameter value takes precedence.\
For example, in the following code, we update the parameterized 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 speak[]()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function to take an argument with a parameterized type. Although the function has a default parameter value for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 a
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , Mojo instead uses the inferred 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 a
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter value from the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 bar
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument (as written, the default 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 a
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value can never be used, but this is just for demonstration purposes):\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @fieldwise_init\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Bar\cf5 [\cf14 v\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 pass\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  speak\cf5 [\cf14 a\cf5 :\cf14  Int \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 3\cf5 ,\cf14  msg\cf5 :\cf14  String \cf5 =\cf14  \cf19 "woof"\cf5 ](\cf14 bar\cf5 :\cf14  Bar\cf5 [\cf14 a\cf5 ]):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 msg\cf5 ,\cf14  a\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 use_inferred\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     speak\cf5 (\cf14 Bar\cf5 [\cf16 9\cf5 ]())\cf14   \cf6 # prints 'woof 9'\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 As mentioned above, you can also use optional parameters and keyword parameters in a struct:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 KwParamStruct\cf5 [\cf14 greeting\cf5 :\cf14  String \cf5 =\cf14  \cf19 "Hello"\cf5 ,\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String \cf5 =\cf14  \cf19 "\uc0\u55357 \u56613 mojo\u55357 \u56613 "\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf14 Self\cf5 .\cf14 greeting\cf5 ,\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 name\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 use_kw_params\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  a \cf5 =\cf14  KwParamStruct\cf5 []()\cf14                  \cf6 # prints 'Hello \uc0\u55357 \u56613 mojo\u55357 \u56613 '\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  b \cf5 =\cf14  KwParamStruct\cf5 [\cf14 name\cf5 =\cf19 "World"\cf5 ]()\cf14      \cf6 # prints 'Hello World'\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  c \cf5 =\cf14  KwParamStruct\cf5 [\cf14 greeting\cf5 =\cf19 "Hola"\cf5 ]()\cf14   \cf6 # prints 'Hola \uc0\u55357 \u56613 mojo\u55357 \u56613 '\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 Mojo supports positional-only and keyword-only parameters, following the same rules as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/functions/#positional-only-and-keyword-only-arguments"}}{\fldrslt \ul positional-only and keyword-only arguments}}.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Variadic parameters\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo also supports variadic parameters, similar to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/functions/#variadic-arguments"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Variadic arguments}}:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyTensor\cf5 [*\cf14 dimensions\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 pass\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Variadic parameters currently have some limitations that variadic arguments don't have:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Variadic parameters must be homogeneous\'97that is, all the values must be the same type.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The parameter type must be register-passable.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The parameter values aren't automatically projected into a list, so you need to construct the list (in this case, a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 VariadicParamList
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) explicitly:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  sum_params\cf5 [*\cf14 values\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ]()\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  Int\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf14 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
    \cf5 comptime\cf14  \cf15 list\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  VariadicParamList\cf5 [*\cf14 values\cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\ls6\ilvl0\cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
    \cf5 var\cf14  \cf15 sum\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 0\cf14 \cb1 \
\ls6\ilvl0\cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
    \cf5 for\cf14  v \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 list\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\ls6\ilvl0\cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
        \cf15 sum\cf14  \cf5 +=\cf14  v\cb1 \
\ls6\ilvl0\cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
    \cf5 return\cf14  \cf15 sum\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf17 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf18 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Variadic keyword parameters (for example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 **kwparams
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) are not supported yet.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Infer-only parameters\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Sometimes you need to declare functions where parameters depend on other parameters. Because the signature is processed left to right, a parameter can only depend on a parameter earlier in the parameter list. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  dependent_type\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 :\cf14  DType\cf5 ,\cf14  value\cf5 :\cf14  Scalar\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 ]]():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf19 "Value: "\cf5 ,\cf14  value\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf19 "Value is floating-point: "\cf5 ,\cf14  dtype\cf5 .\cf14 is_floating_point\cf5 ())\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13 dependent_type\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 float64\cf5 ,\cf14  Float64\cf5 (\cf16 2.2\cf5 )]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 Value:  2.2000000000000002\cb1 \
\cb13 Value is floating-point:  True\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can't reverse the position of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 dtype
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 value
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameters, because 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 value
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  depends on 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 dtype
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . However, because 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 dtype
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a required parameter, you can't leave it out of the parameter list and let Mojo infer it from 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 value
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 dependent_type\cf5 [\cf14 Float64\cf5 (\cf16 2.2\cf5 )]()\cf14  \cf6 # Error!\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Infer-only parameters are a special class of parameters that are always either inferred from context or specified by keyword. Infer-only parameters are placed at the beginning of the parameter list, set off from other parameters by the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 //
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  sigil:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  example\cf5 [\cf14 T\cf5 :\cf14  Copyable\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 //,\cf14  \cf15 list\cf5 :\cf14  List\cf5 [\cf14 T\cf5 ]]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Transforming 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 dtype
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  into an infer-only parameter solves this problem:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  dependent_type\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 :\cf14  DType\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 //,\cf14  value\cf5 :\cf14  Scalar\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 ]]():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf19 "Value: "\cf5 ,\cf14  value\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf19 "Value is floating-point: "\cf5 ,\cf14  dtype\cf5 .\cf14 is_floating_point\cf5 ())\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13 dependent_type\cf5 [\cf14 Float64\cf5 (\cf16 2.2\cf5 )]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 Value:  2.2000000000000002\cb1 \
\cb13 Value is floating-point:  True\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Because infer-only parameters are declared at the beginning of the parameter list, other parameters can depend on them, and the compiler will always attempt to infer the infer-only values from bound parameters or arguments.\
There are sometimes cases where it's useful to specify an infer-only parameter by keyword. For example, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/span/Span/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Span}} type is parameterized on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/lifetimes/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul origin}}:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Span\cf5 [mut:\cf14  Bool\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 //,\cf14  T\cf5 :\cf14  Copyable\cf5 ,\cf14  origin\cf5 :\cf14  Origin\cf5 [mut]]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # ... implementation omitted\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mut
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter is infer-only. The value is usually inferred when you create an instance of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Span
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Binding the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mut
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter by keyword lets you define a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Span
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that requires a mutable origin.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 mutate_span\cf5 (\cf14 span\cf5 :\cf14  Span\cf5 [mut=\cf16 True\cf5 ,\cf14  Byte\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 ...])\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 range\cf5 (\cf16 0\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf15 len\cf5 (\cf14 span\cf5 ),\cf14  \cf16 2\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 if\cf14  i \cf5 +\cf14  \cf16 1\cf14  \cf5 <\cf14  \cf15 len\cf5 (\cf14 span\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             span\cf5 .\cf14 swap_elements\cf5 (\cf14 i\cf5 ,\cf14  i \cf5 +\cf14  \cf16 1\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If the compiler can't infer the value of an infer-only parameter, and it's not specified by keyword, compilation fails.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Parameter expressions are just Mojo code\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A parameter expression is any code expression (such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 a+b
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) that occurs where a parameter is expected. Parameter expressions support operators and function calls, just like runtime code, and all parameter types use the same type system as the runtime program (such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
Because parameter expressions use the same grammar and types as runtime Mojo code, you can use many {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dependent_type"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul "dependent type"}} features. For example, you might want to define a helper function to concatenate two SIMD vectors:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  concat\cf5 [\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     dtype\cf5 :\cf14  DType\cf5 ,\cf14  ls_size\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  rh_size\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 //\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 ](\cf14 lhs\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  ls_size\cf5 ],\cf14  rhs\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  rh_size\cf5 ])\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  ls_size \cf5 +\cf14  rh_size\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  result \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  ls_size \cf5 +\cf14  rh_size\cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  \cf5 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 range\cf5 (\cf14 ls_size\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         result\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  lhs\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  \cf5 for\cf14  j \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 range\cf5 (\cf14 rh_size\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         result\cf5 [\cf14 ls_size \cf5 +\cf14  j\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  rhs\cf5 [\cf14 j\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  result\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Note that the resulting length is the sum of the input vector lengths, and this is expressed with a simple 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 +
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Powerful compile-time programming\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 While simple expressions are useful, sometimes you want to write imperative compile-time logic with control flow. You can even do compile-time recursion. For instance, here is an example "tree reduction" algorithm that sums all elements of a vector recursively into a scalar:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 slice\cf5 [\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     dtype\cf5 :\cf14  DType\cf5 ,\cf14  size\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 //\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 ](\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  size\cf5 ],\cf14  offset\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 )\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  size \cf5 //\cf14  \cf16 2\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  new_size \cf5 =\cf14  size \cf5 //\cf14  \cf16 2\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  result \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  new_size\cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 range\cf5 (\cf14 new_size\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         result\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 1\cf5 ](\cf14 x\cf5 [\cf14 i \cf5 +\cf14  offset\cf5 ])\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  result\cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 reduce_add\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 )\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  Int\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  \cf5 if\cf14  x\cf5 .\cf14 size \cf5 ==\cf14  \cf16 1\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 return\cf14  Int\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 [\cf16 0\cf5 ])\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 elif\cf14  x\cf5 .\cf14 size \cf5 ==\cf14  \cf16 2\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 return\cf14  Int\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 [\cf16 0\cf5 ])\cf14  \cf5 +\cf14  Int\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 [\cf16 1\cf5 ])\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf6 # Extract the top/bottom halves, add them, sum the elements.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  half_size \cf5 =\cf14  x\cf5 .\cf14 size \cf5 //\cf14  \cf16 2\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  lhs \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 slice\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 0\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  rhs \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 slice\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 ,\cf14  half_size\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  reduce_add\cf5 (\cf14 lhs \cf5 +\cf14  rhs\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  x \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf15 int\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 4\cf5 ](\cf16 1\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 2\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 3\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 4\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 x\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf19 "Elements sum:"\cf5 ,\cf14  reduce_add\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 [1, 2, 3, 4]\cb1 \
\cb13 Elements sum: 10\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 This makes use of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/metaprogramming/comptime-evaluation/#comptime-if"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul comptime if}} statement, which is an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement that runs at compile-time. It requires that its condition be a valid parameter expression, and ensures that only the live branch of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement is compiled into the program. This is similar to use of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loop shown earlier.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Parameterized 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  values\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A parameterized 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value is a compile-time expression that takes a list of parameters and returns a compile-time constant value:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  AddOne\cf5 [\cf14 a\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 :\cf14  Int \cf5 =\cf14  a \cf5 +\cf14  \cf16 1\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  nine \cf5 =\cf14  AddOne\cf5 [\cf16 8\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 As you can see in the previous example, a parameterized 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value is a little like a compile-time-only function. A regular function or method can also be invoked at compile time:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 add_one\cf5 (\cf14 a\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 )\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  Int\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  a \cf5 +\cf14  \cf16 1\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  ten \cf5 =\cf14  add_one\cf5 (\cf16 9\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 A major difference between a function and a parameterized 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value is that the value of a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  expression can be a type, while a function can't return a type as a value.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb13 # Does not work\'97-dynamic type values not permitted\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 int_type\cf5 ()\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  AnyType\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb13 # Works\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  IntType \cf5 =\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Because a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value can be a type, you can use parameterized 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values to express new types:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  TwoOfAKind\cf5 [\cf14 dt\cf5 :\cf14  DType\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 dt\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 2\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 twoFloats \cf5 =\cf14  TwoOfAKind\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 float32\cf5 ](\cf16 1.0\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 2.0\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  StringKeyDict\cf5 [\cf14 ValueType\cf5 :\cf14  Copyable \cf5 &\cf14  ImplicitlyDestructible\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  Dict\cf5 [\cf14 String\cf5 ,\cf14  ValueType\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  b\cf5 :\cf14  StringKeyDict\cf5 [\cf14 UInt8\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  \cf5 \{\cf19 "answer"\cf5 :\cf14  \cf16 42\cf5 \}\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Parameterized 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  declarations support the same features as parameterized structs or functions: infer-only parameters, keyword-only and optional parameters, \cf5 \ul automatic parameterization\cf2 \ulnone , and so on.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  Floats\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  half_width\cf5 :\cf14  Bool \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 False\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 (\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 float16 \cf5 if\cf14  half_width \cf5 else\cf14  DType\cf5 .\cf14 float32\cf5 ),\cf14  size\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  floats \cf5 =\cf14  Floats\cf5 [\cf16 2\cf5 ](\cf16 6.0\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 8.0\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  half_floats \cf5 =\cf14  Floats\cf5 [\cf16 2\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 True\cf5 ](\cf16 10.0\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 12.0\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Fully-bound, partially-bound, and unbound types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A parameterized type with its parameters specified is said to be fully-bound. That is, all of its parameters are bound to values. As mentioned before, you can only instantiate a fully-bound type (sometimes called a concrete type).\
However, parameterized types can be unbound or partially bound in some contexts. For example, you can use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to create a type alias to a partially-bound type to create a new type that requires fewer parameters:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  StringKeyDict \cf5 =\cf14  Dict\cf5 [\cf14 String\cf5 ,\cf14  _\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  b\cf5 :\cf14  StringKeyDict\cf5 [\cf14 UInt8\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  \cf5 \{\cf19 "answer"\cf5 :\cf14  \cf16 42\cf5 \}\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringKeyDict
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a type alias for a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Dict
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that takes 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keys. The underscore 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 _
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in the parameter list indicates that the second parameter, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 V
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (the value type), is unbound. You specify the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 V
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter later, when you use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringKeyDict
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb20 Partially-bound types versus parameterized comptime values\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb20 You may notice that this example is very similar to an example in the section on \ul parameterized 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb21 comptime
\fs28 \cb20  values\ulnone . For simple type aliases like this, you can use either a partially-bound type or a parameterized 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 comptime
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  value. Parameterized 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 comptime
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  values provide a more flexible way to define type aliases, since you can define the order of the parameters, add default values, and so on.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb20 Partially-bound and unbound types can provide a handy shortcut when defining parameterized functions and 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 comptime
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  values, called \ul automatic parameterization\ulnone .\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can also use partially-bound types as the type bound for an argument or parameter.\
For example, given the following type:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyType\cf5 [\cf14 s\cf5 :\cf14  String\cf5 ,\cf14  i\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  i2\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  b\cf5 :\cf14  Bool \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 True\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 pass\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 It can appear in code in the following forms:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Fully bound, with all of its parameters specified:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 my_fn1\cf5 (\cf14 m1\cf5 :\cf14  MyType\cf5 [\cf19 "Hello"\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 3\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 4\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 True\cf5 ])\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf14 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
    \cf5 pass\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf17 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf18 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Partially bound, with some but not all of its parameters specified:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 my_fn2\cf5 (\cf14 m2\cf5 :\cf14  MyType\cf5 [\cf19 "Hola"\cf5 ,\cf14  _\cf5 ,\cf14  _\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 True\cf5 ])\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf14 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
    \cf5 pass\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf17 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf18 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Unbound, with no parameters specified:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 my_fn3\cf5 (\cf14 m3\cf5 :\cf14  MyType\cf5 [\cf14 _\cf5 ,\cf14  _\cf5 ,\cf14  _\cf5 ,\cf14  _\cf5 ])\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf14 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
    \cf5 pass\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf17 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf18 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can also use three dots (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ...
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) to unbind an arbitrary number of parameters at the end of a parameter list (including any keyword-only parameters):\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb13 # These two types are equivalent\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb13 MyType\cf5 [\cf19 "Hello"\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 ...]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 MyType\cf5 [\cf19 "Hello"\cf5 ,\cf14  _\cf5 ,\cf14  _\cf5 ,\cf14  _\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When a parameter is explicitly unbound with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 _
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ...
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  expressions, you must specify a value for that parameter to use the type. The default values of explicitly unbound parameters are ignored.\
Partially-bound and unbound parameterized types can be used in some contexts where the missing (unbound) parameters will be supplied later\'97such as in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/metaprogramming/comptime-evaluation/#comptime-values"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul comptime
\fs32 \cb1  values}} and \cf5 \ul automatically parameterized functions\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Omitted parameters\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo also supports an alternate format for unbound parameters where parameters are simply omitted from the expression:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @fieldwise_init\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyComplicatedType\cf5 [\cf14 a\cf5 :\cf14  Int \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 7\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 /,\cf14  b\cf5 :\cf14  Int \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 8\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 *,\cf14  c\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  d\cf5 :\cf14  Int \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 9\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 pass\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb13 # Unbound\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 my_func\cf5 (\cf14 t\cf5 :\cf14  MyComplicatedType\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 pass\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This is equivalent to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 def my_func(t: MyComplicatedType[...]): pass
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . That is, all parameters (positional-only, positional-or-keyword, keyword-only) are unbound and their default values (if any) ignored.\
Note that when an argument type is partially bound, default values will be bound:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb13 # Partially bound\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb13 MyComplicatedType\cf5 [\cf16 1\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb13 # Equivalent to\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb13 MyComplicatedType\cf5 [\cf16 1\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 8\cf5 ,\cf14  c\cf5 =\cf14 _\cf5 ,\cf14  d\cf5 =\cf16 9\cf5 ]\cf14   \cf6 # Uses default values for `b` and `d`.\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 This behavior with omitted parameters is currently supported for backwards compatibility. We intend to reconcile the behavior of omitted parameters and explicitly unbound parameters in the future.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Automatic parameterization\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo supports "automatic" parameterization of functions and parameterized 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values. If a function argument type or parameter type is \cf5 \ul partially-bound or unbound\cf2 \ulnone , the unbound parameters are automatically added as parameters on the function. This is easier to understand with an example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 print_params\cf5 (\cf14 vec\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 vec\cf5 .\cf14 dtype\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 vec\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  v \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 float64\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 4\cf5 ](\cf16 1.0\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 2.0\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 3.0\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 4.0\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 print_params\cf5 (\cf14 v\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 float64\cb1 \
\cb13 4\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In the above example, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 print_params()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function is automatically parameterized. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 vec
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument takes an argument of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD[...]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . This is an \cf5 \ul unbound parameterized type\cf2 \ulnone \'97that is, it doesn't specify any parameter values for the type. Mojo treats the unbound parameters on 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 vec
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as infer-only parameters on the function. This is roughly equivalent to the following code:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  print_params2\cf5 [\cf14 t\cf5 :\cf14  DType\cf5 ,\cf14  s\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 //](\cf14 vec\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 t\cf5 ,\cf14  s\cf5 ]):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 vec\cf5 .\cf14 dtype\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 vec\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When you call 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 print_params()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  you must pass it a concrete instance of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type\'97that is, one with all of its parameters specified, like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD[DType.float64, 4]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The Mojo compiler infers the parameter values from the input argument.\
With a manually parameterized function, you can access the parameters by name (for example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 t
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 s
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in the previous example), which is not an option in an automatically parameterized function.\
However, you can always access a type's parameters and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members using dot syntax (for example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 vec.dtype
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ), as described in \cf5 \ul Referencing struct parameters\cf2 \ulnone . This ability to access a type's parameters and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members is not specific to automatically parameterized functions, you can use it anywhere.\
You can even use this syntax in the function's signature to define a function's arguments and return type based on an argument's parameters or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members.\
For example, if you want your function to take two SIMD vectors with the same type and size, you can write code like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 interleave\cf5 (\cf14 v1\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 ,\cf14  v2\cf5 :\cf14  type_of\cf5 (\cf14 v1\cf5 ))\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 v1\cf5 .\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  v1\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 *\cf16 2\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  result \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 v1\cf5 .\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  v1\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 *\cf16 2\cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 range\cf5 (\cf14 v1\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         result\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 *\cf16 2\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 v1\cf5 .\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 1\cf5 ](\cf14 v1\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 ])\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         result\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 *\cf16 2\cf5 +\cf16 1\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 v1\cf5 .\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 1\cf5 ](\cf14 v2\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 ])\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  result\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  a \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 int16\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 4\cf5 ](\cf16 1\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 2\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 3\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 4\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  b \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 int16\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 4\cf5 ](\cf16 0\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 0\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 0\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 0\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  c \cf5 =\cf14  interleave\cf5 (\cf14 a\cf5 ,\cf14  b\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 print(\cf14 c\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 [1, 0, 2, 0, 3, 0, 4, 0]\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 As shown in the example, you can use the magic 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 type_of(x)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  call if you just want to match the type of an argument. In this case, it's more convenient and compact than writing the equivalent 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD[v1.dtype, v1.size]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Automatic parameterization of parameters\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can also take advantage of automatic parameterization in the parameter list of a function or parameterized 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  foo\cf5 [\cf14 value\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 ]():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 pass\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb13 # Equivalent to:\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  foo\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 :\cf14  DType\cf5 ,\cf14  size\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 //,\cf14  value\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  size\cf5 ]]():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 pass\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here's another example using a parameterized 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  Foo\cf5 [\cf14 S\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  Bar\cf5 [\cf14 S\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb13 # Equivalent to:\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  Foo\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 :\cf14  DType\cf5 ,\cf14  size\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 //,\cf14  S\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 dtype\cf5 ,\cf14  size\cf5 ]]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  Bar\cf5 [\cf14 S\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Automatic parameterization with partially-bound types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo also supports automatic parameterization: with \cf5 \ul partially-bound parameterized types\cf2 \ulnone  (that is, types with some but not all of the parameters specified).\
For example, suppose we have a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Fudge
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct with three parameters:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @fieldwise_init\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Fudge\cf5 [\cf14 sugar\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  cream\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  chocolate\cf5 :\cf14  Int \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 7\cf5 ](\cf14 Writable\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 pass\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 We can write a function that takes a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Fudge
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument with just one bound parameter (it's partially bound):\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 eat\cf5 (\cf14 f\cf5 :\cf14  Fudge\cf5 [\cf16 5\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 ...]):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf19 "Ate "\cf14  \cf5 +\cf14  String\cf5 (\cf14 f\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 eat()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function takes a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Fudge
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct with the first parameter (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 sugar
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) bound to the value 5. The second and third parameters, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 cream
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 chocolate
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are unbound.\
The unbound 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 cream
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 chocolate
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameters become implicit parameters on the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 eat
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function. In practice, this is roughly equivalent to writing:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  eat\cf5 [\cf14 cr\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  ch\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ](\cf14 f\cf5 :\cf14  Fudge\cf5 [\cf16 5\cf5 ,\cf14  cr\cf5 ,\cf14  ch\cf5 ]):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf19 "Ate"\cf5 ,\cf14  String\cf5 (\cf14 f\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In both cases, we can call the function by passing in an instance with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 cream
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 chocolate
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameters bound:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 eat\cf5 (\cf14 Fudge\cf5 [\cf16 5\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 5\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 7\cf5 ]())\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 eat\cf5 (\cf14 Fudge\cf5 [\cf16 5\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 8\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 9\cf5 ]())\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 Ate Fudge (5,5,7)\cb1 \
\cb13 Ate Fudge (5,8,9)\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If you try to pass in an argument with a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 sugar
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value other than 5, compilation fails, because it doesn't match the argument type:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 eat\cf5 (\cf14 Fudge\cf5 [\cf16 12\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 5\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 7\cf5 ]())\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb13 # ERROR: invalid call to 'eat': argument #0 cannot be converted from 'Fudge[12, 5, 7]' to 'Fudge[5, 5, 7]'\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can also explicitly unbind individual parameters. This gives you more freedom in specifying unbound parameters.\
For example, you might want to let the user specify values for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 sugar
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 chocolate
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and leave 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 cream
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constant. To do this, replace each unbound parameter value with a single underscore (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 _
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ):\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 devour\cf5 (\cf14 f\cf5 :\cf14  Fudge\cf5 [\cf14 _\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 6\cf5 ,\cf14  _\cf5 ]):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf19 "Devoured"\cf5 ,\cf14   String\cf5 (\cf14 f\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Again, the unbound parameters (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 sugar
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 chocolate
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) are added as implicit parameters on the function. This version is roughly equivalent to the following code, where these two values are explicitly bound to the input parameters, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 su
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ch
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  devour\cf5 [\cf14 su\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  ch\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ](\cf14 f\cf5 :\cf14  Fudge\cf5 [\cf14 su\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 6\cf5 ,\cf14  ch\cf5 ]):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf19 "Devoured"\cf5 ,\cf14  String\cf5 (\cf14 f\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can also specify parameters by keyword, or mix positional and keyword parameters, so the following function is roughly equivalent to the previous one: the first parameter, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 sugar
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is explicitly unbound with the underscore character. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 chocolate
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter is unbound using the keyword syntax, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 chocolate=_
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . And 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 cream
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is explicitly bound to the value 6:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 devour\cf5 (\cf14 f\cf5 :\cf14  Fudge\cf5 [\cf14 _\cf5 ,\cf14  chocolate\cf5 =\cf14 _\cf5 ,\cf14  cream\cf5 =\cf16 6\cf5 ]):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf19 "Devoured"\cf5 ,\cf14  String\cf5 (\cf14 f\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 All three versions of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 devour()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function work with the following calls:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 devour\cf5 (\cf14 Fudge\cf5 [\cf16 3\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 6\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 9\cf5 ]())\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 devour\cf5 (\cf14 Fudge\cf5 [\cf16 4\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 6\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 8\cf5 ]())\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 Devoured Fudge (3,6,9)\cb1 \
\cb13 Devoured Fudge (4,6,8)\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 The 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 rebind()
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  builtin\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 One of the consequences of Mojo not performing function instantiation in the parser like C++ is that Mojo cannot always figure out whether some parameterized types are equal and complain about an invalid conversion. This typically occurs in static dispatch patterns. For example, the following code won't compile:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 take_simd8\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 float32\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 8\cf5 ]):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 pass\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  generic_simd\cf5 [\cf14 nelts\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ](\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 float32\cf5 ,\cf14  nelts\cf5 ]):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  \cf5 if\cf14  nelts \cf5 ==\cf14  \cf16 8\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         take_simd8\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The parser will complain:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 error: invalid call to 'take_simd8': argument #0 cannot be converted from\cb1 \
\cb13 'SIMD[f32, nelts]' to 'SIMD[f32, 8]'\cb1 \
\cb13         take_simd8(x)\cb1 \
\cb13         ~~~~~~~~~~^~~\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This is because the parser fully type-checks the function without instantiation, and the type of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is still 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD[f32, nelts]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and not 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD[f32, 8]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , despite the static conditional. The remedy is to manually "rebind" the type of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 rebind
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  builtin, which inserts a compile-time assert that the input and result types resolve to the same type after function instantiation:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 take_simd8\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 float32\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 8\cf5 ]):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 pass\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  generic_simd\cf5 [\cf14 nelts\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ](\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 float32\cf5 ,\cf14  nelts\cf5 ]):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  \cf5 if\cf14  nelts \cf5 ==\cf14  \cf16 8\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         take_simd8\cf5 (\cf14 rebind\cf5 [\cf14 SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 float32\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 8\cf5 ]](\cf14 x\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

